In this phase, the exact needs of the system were defined. Functionally, the system must allow an administrator to register students and teachers, capture and store facial data, mark attendance automatically from a live camera feed, let teachers view and edit records, and export reports as PDF or Excel files. Non-functionally, it should achieve recognition accuracy above ninety percent, respond within three seconds, store personal data in encrypted form, work on common browsers such as Chrome, Edge, and Firefox, and support at least five hundred users. The hardware requirement is a laptop with eight gigabytes of RAM and a webcam, while the software stack includes Python, OpenCV, Flask or Django, MySQL or SQLite, and HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. A feasibility study confirmed that the project is technically possible because open-source tools are available, economically viable because the cost is minimal, and operationally practical because staff can use it with little training.
